A: You are victim of false sharing in the second approach. Here an interesting article from Intel about that. False sharing occurs when threads on different processors modify
  variables that reside on the same cache line. This invalidates the
  cache line and forces a memory update to maintain cache coherency. If two processors operate on independent data in the same memory
  address region storable in a single line, the cache coherency
  mechanisms in the system may force the whole line across the bus or
  interconnect with every data write, forcing memory stalls in addition
  to wasting system bandwidth Intuitively, I don't think the first approach should be slower. You indeed create a private copy on each thread and then apply the final result in a global variable. The behaviour is somehow the same with your shared array, but the problem here is even if your accesses are independent, you get false sharing.

 You must implement the following function 
Function name: calculate_pi
Parameters: int argc, char **argv
Return type: int

Brief description:
Estimates the value of π using the Monte Carlo simulation method with OpenMP parallelization. The number of iterations N is calculated as 256 × 256 × the command-line input. For each iteration, it generates a random point (x, y) in the unit square and checks if it lies inside the unit circle. A parallel for-loop with reduction accumulates the count of points inside the circle. It then computes π as 4 * (points inside) / N, calculates the relative error compared to the actual value of π (acos(-1)), and measures the total execution time in milliseconds. The results are printed to the console.

code_gens.code:
#include <omp.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <math.h>

#define PI 3.14159265358979323846
#define PREC 256

int calculate_pi(int argc, char **argv) {
    double start_time = omp_get_wtime();
    int nthreads = omp_get_max_threads();
    int num_iterations = atoi(argv[1]);
    int counter[8];
    int sum = 0;

    #pragma omp parallel private(seed, x, y, i, nproc)
    {
        seed = 25234 + 17 * omp_get_thread_num();
        nproc = omp_get_thread_num();
        #pragma omp parallel for
        for(i=0; i<num_iterations/8; i++){
            x = (double)rand_r(&seed) / RAND_MAX;
            y = (double)rand_r(&seed) / RAND_MAX;
            if(x*x+y*y<1){
                counter[nproc]++;
            } 
        }
    }

    for(int i=0; i<8; i++){
        sum+=counter[i];
    }

    double pi_estimate = 4 * (double)sum / num_iterations;
    double relative_error = fabs(pi_estimate - PI);
    double time_taken = omp_get_wtime() - start_time;
    printf("Estimated Pi: %f\n", pi_estimate);
    printf("Relative Error: %f\n", relative_error);
    printf("Time Taken: %.2f ms\n", time_taken * 1000);

    return 0;
}

