 to keep the physical cores constantly busy, but actually this is not necessarily a win situation: if the code is purely CPU bound, running more than N threads does not help because the physical core are already fully busy with N threads if the code is purely memory-bound, running more than N threads doesn't help either (*) because at the end the bottleneck is the bandwith: the threads will be interleaved on the cores, but each one will receive twice as less data and will be active twice as less time. the only case where it can help is if the different threads are not all CPU-bound or memory-bound at the same time (*) this can even hurt instead of helping, because more threads often means more random accesses to the memory, hence more cache misses. This very visible on the performance curves of the other answer. It may sound a bit restrictive, but actually the logical cores are useful anyway because the OS can schedule the threads from the processes other than your main compute process (that is, mostly system processes in a HPC context) without necessarily having to switch your computing threads.

